course: dictionary
lesson: 6934
title: A padlock is not a promise
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A padlock is a lock you can take off and move. It has a curved metal bar, called a shackle, that clicks into the body of the lock. You open it with a key or a combination. Bike chains, school lockers and garden gates often have padlocks.
explain: To padlock something means to lock it with a padlock. Look at the clues in «a combination padlock» and «padlock the gate»: the first word is a noun, a thing you can hold, and the second is a verb, an action you do.
explain: On a website, a small padlock picture means the connection is secure. The clue words are «encrypted» and «scrambled», which means others cannot easily read it. But the sentence goes on: a padlock only means the connection is private, not that the site is honest. A padlock is not a promise that the website is true.
fact: A padlock is a lock you can take off and move, opened with a key or a combination.
fact: To padlock something means to lock it with a padlock, as in «padlock the gate».
fact: The shackle is the curved metal bar that clicks into the body of the padlock.
fact: A padlock picture on a website means the connection is secure and scrambled, not that the site is honest.
fact: A smart reader checks who made a website and whether other good sources agree.
exercise: To padlock the gate means what? | To paint it bright red | To lose the key | *To lock it with a padlock | To climb over it
exercise: What is the curved metal bar on a padlock called? | The hinge | The handle | The keyhole | *The shackle
exercise: A padlock picture on a website means what? | The site is definitely honest and true | The site was made by a teacher | The site is free to use | *The connection is secure and scrambled
test: What opens a combination padlock? | A key | *A set of numbers | A magnet | Shaking it hard
test: Why can a scam site still have a padlock? | Padlocks can only be made of gold | Because padlocks stop all viruses | *Because a padlock only shows the connection is private, not that the site is honest | Scam sites never have padlocks
exam: What is the best way to check a website is honest? | *Check who made it and whether other good sources agree | Click every link quickly | Trust any site with nice colours | Look only for a padlock picture
